  "notes": [
    "[GENERAL] Unlike most Indian black teas made from the robust *Camellia sinensis* var. *assamica*, Darjeeling tea is traditionally cultivated from the smaller-leaf Chinese variety (*Camellia sinensis* var. *sinensis*), yielding a lighter, more aromatic cup often called the \"Champagne of Teas\".",
    "[GENERAL] Darjeeling leaves are more delicate than typical black teas and extract best using water heated just below a rolling boil, around 85°C to 95°C (185°F to 203°F), to prevent excess bitterness.",
    "[GENERAL] A standard brewing ratio for loose-leaf Darjeeling is approximately 2 to 2.5 grams of tea per 200 to 250 ml (about 8 oz) of hot water.",
    "[GENERAL] While black teas are frequently paired with milk, whole-leaf Darjeeling is traditionally served plain or with a light touch of lemon to preserve its nuanced floral and muscatel flavor notes.",
    "[GENERAL] Quality orthodox loose-leaf Darjeeling can often be infused a second time by extending the steep duration by 30 to 60 seconds."
  ],
